ght the code that people think  of as Android and so we'll spend some  time thinking about that then we'll get  into services things that are delivered  as applications most of them by Google  but also by third parties that add  additional security protections and then  the last is what we've done to work with  other parties in the ecosystem to make  sure that friction that can sometimes  impede security doesn't get in the way  of security being delivered and in  particular around  dates and how we're enabling that so my  background for people who are curious is  in creation and delivery of exploits  going back quite a ways and so I was  sort of on the more offensive side of  security and one of the things that was  really interesting from the offensive  side of security is if you look at  security primitives basically 35-ish  years ago the US government put together  something called the orange book and  there are similar types of things that  were put together by other governments  they're been put together in other  places as well but there's a fairly  well-defined set of expected security  primitives that would exist on a secured  device and what's interesting is it's  taken a really long time for the  software industry to evolve to a point  where it's actually providing all those  primitives when I think about Android  and it's sort of phase in that evolution  I put it right next to what I think of  as the other mobile operating system so  Android iOS Chrome OS all uniformly  provide the same primitives which was  the ones that were described almost 35  years ago an orange book and these other  manuals but that just took a really long  time for them to get broadly adopted and  those are isolation of applications  making sure that there was  cryptographically strong device  integrity that was rooted in a hardware  root of trust then there was something  interesting that happened in those  intervening 30 years which was the  realization that there were bugs in  s